# Native fixture: one module's refused cross-module call must be THAT
# module's compile error, not the death of the codegen worker.
#
# `abi_feeder.Feeder.feed` passes an f64 value to `Sink.mix(n: i64)` across
# modules. Cranelift refuses it (correctly; a float never narrows to an int
# implicitly, so the trigger survives any int -> f64 widening rule):
#   E-BACKEND-CRANELIFT-CALL-ABI: abi_sink.Sink.mix: call in
#   abi_feeder.Feeder.feed: arg 1 is f64 but the callee declares i64;
#   convert it explicitly at the call site
#
# Before 2026-11-14 that refusal was a panic: `native-build` died with
# SIGABRT (exit 134) and, in the sharded Stage 3 lane, every module of the
# build was marked failed. Expected now, with SIMPLE_NATIVE_GO_TO_END=1 and
# `native-build <main.spl> --backend=cranelift --entry-closure` from a copy of
# this directory outside test/ (closure builds exclude fixture trees): the
# build exits non-zero WITHOUT a signal, names abi_feeder as the one failed
# module with the message above, and still compiles abi_sink, abi_good and
# main. If the per-file worker pool refuses for lack of memory, add
# SIMPLE_BOOTSTRAP_WHOLE_PROGRAM=1.
use abi_sink.{Sink}
use abi_feeder.{Feeder}
use abi_good.{good_twice}

fn main():
    var s = Sink.create()
    var f = Feeder.create()
    f.feed(s)
    print "n={f.n} twice={good_twice(21)}"
